    29	---
    30	## Definition
    31	
    32	Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]).
    33	
    34	- An **open cover** of $(X,\mathcal{T})$ is a family
    35	  $\mathcal{U} \subseteq \mathcal{T}$ of open sets with $X = \bigcup \mathcal{U}$,
    36	  where $\bigcup \mathcal{U} = \{\, x \in X : x \in U \text{ for some } U \in \mathcal{U} \,\}$.
    37	- A **subcover** of $\mathcal{U}$ is a subfamily $\mathcal{V} \subseteq \mathcal{U}$
    38	  that is itself an open cover.
    39	- A family $\mathcal{V}$ of sets is **finite** when $\mathcal{V} = \varnothing$ or
    40	  there are $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and sets $V_0, \dots, V_n$ with
    41	  $\mathcal{V} = \{V_0, \dots, V_n\}$; repetitions in the list are allowed and
    42	  harmless.
    43	- $(X,\mathcal{T})$ is **compact** when every open cover of it has a finite
    44	  subcover: for every open cover $\mathcal{U}$, either $X = \varnothing$ and the
    45	  empty subfamily covers it, or there are $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and
    46	  $U_0, \dots, U_n \in \mathcal{U}$ with
    47	  $$X = U_0 \cup \dots \cup U_n .$$
    48	- A subset $A \subseteq X$ is a **compact subset** of $X$ when the subspace
    49	  $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ is a compact topological space, $\mathcal{T}_A$ being the
    50	  subspace topology ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]).
    51	
    52	**Compactness of a subset is defined intrinsically, and only intrinsically.**
    53	The last clause speaks about the subspace $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ and its own open
    54	sets, not about families of open subsets of the ambient $X$. The two readings do
    55	agree, but that is a theorem and not a convention: it is
    56	[[lem-compactness-of-a-subspace-is-ambient]], and no item of this library may use
    57	the ambient reading without citing it. Taking the intrinsic reading makes
    58	compactness a property of the topological space $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ alone.
    59	Hence it is preserved when $A$ is embedded homeomorphically as a subspace, or
    60	when another ambient space induces the same topology on $A$; it need not be
    61	preserved if the induced topology changes. This is exactly the convention already fixed for
    62	metric spaces by [[def-metric-compactness]], and the agreement of that
    63	definition with this one is [[thm-compactness-agrees-with-metric-compactness]].
    64	
    65	**The empty space is compact**, since the empty subfamily of any family covers
    66	it; this is the reason the clause above is written with the two cases. Every
    67	space listed as $\{x_0, \dots, x_n\}$ is compact too: given a cover, each $x_i$
    68	lies in some member, and finitely many members named in this way already cover.
    69	So every finite space is compact, whatever its topology, and in particular the
    70	discrete topology on a finite set is compact while the discrete topology on an
    71	infinite set is not ([[def-standard-topologies]]).
    72	
    73	**The finiteness convention.** "Finite" above is the listing form. It agrees with
    74	the definition of finiteness by equinumerosity with a natural number
    75	([[def-countable]]), in both directions, and the agreement is the one already
    76	discharged in [[def-metric-compactness]]: a nonempty set equinumerous with
    77	$m \ge 1$ is listable, and a set listed as $\{a_0, \dots, a_n\}$ injects into
    78	$\sigma(n)$ by sending $x$ to the least $i \le n$ with $a_i = x$. Neither
    79	direction uses a choice principle; the second selects nothing, taking a least
    80	index instead.
    81	
    82	**Quasicompact is not used here.** Some authors, following Bourbaki, reserve
    83	*compact* for a space that is both compact in the above sense and Hausdorff, and
    84	call the open-cover condition alone *quasicompact*. This library follows the more
    85	widely adopted convention: **compact** means the open-cover condition and nothing
    86	more, and a Hausdorff hypothesis is always written out. The fork is recorded in
    87	[[rem-compactness-conventions-and-choice-ledger]].
    88	
    89	## Remarks
    90	
    91	**Why open covers rather than covers by arbitrary sets.** Nothing in the
    92	definition would break if $\mathcal{U}$ were allowed to consist of arbitrary
    93	subsets of $X$, but the resulting notion would be uninteresting: every space is
    94	covered by its singletons, and only a finite space would survive. Openness of the
    95	members is what makes the condition a genuine restriction, and it is what
    96	[[lem-compactness-of-a-subspace-is-ambient]] has to keep track of when the
    97	ambient space changes.
    98	
    99	**A warning about the word "cover".** A family may cover $A \subseteq X$ without
   100	being a family of subsets of $A$: the members are open subsets of $X$ and their
   101	union merely contains $A$. That is the ambient reading, and it is a different
   102	statement from "$\mathcal{U}$ is an open cover of the space
   103	$(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$", whose members are open subsets of $A$. Which of the two is
   104	meant is written out everywhere on this page.
     1	---
