FILE items/def-metric-compactness.md
34:## Definition
35:
36:Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space ([[def-metric-space]]), with open sets as in
37:[[def-metric-topology]] and balls as in [[def-metric-ball]].
38:
39:- An **open cover** of $(X,d)$ is a family $\mathcal{U}$ of open subsets of $X$
40:  with $X = \bigcup \mathcal{U}$, where
41:  $\bigcup \mathcal{U} = \{\, x \in X : x \in U \text{ for some } U \in \mathcal{U} \,\}$.
42:- A **subcover** of $\mathcal{U}$ is a subfamily
43:  $\mathcal{V} \subseteq \mathcal{U}$ that is itself an open cover.
44:- A family $\mathcal{V}$ of sets is **finite** when $\mathcal{V} = \emptyset$ or
45:  there are $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and sets $V_0, \dots, V_n$ with
46:  $\mathcal{V} = \{V_0, \dots, V_n\}$; repetitions in the list are allowed and
47:  harmless.
48:- $(X,d)$ is **compact** when every open cover of it has a finite subcover: for
49:  every open cover $\mathcal{U}$, either $X = \emptyset$ and the empty subfamily
50:  covers it, or there are $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and
51:  $U_0, \dots, U_n \in \mathcal{U}$ with
52:  $$X = U_0 \cup \dots \cup U_n .$$
53:- A subset $A \subseteq X$ is a **compact subset** of $X$ when the metric
54:  subspace $(A, d_A)$ is a compact metric space, $d_A$ being the restriction of
55:  $d$ to $A \times A$ ([[def-isometry-and-metric-embedding]]).
56:
57:**Compactness of a subset is defined intrinsically, and only intrinsically.**
58:The last clause speaks about the subspace $(A,d_A)$ and its own open sets, not
59:about families of open subsets of the ambient $X$. The two readings do agree,
60:but that is a theorem and not a convention: it is
61:[[lem-compactness-is-intrinsic]], and no item of this library may use the
62:ambient reading without citing it. Taking the intrinsic reading as the
63:definition is what makes "compact" a property of the metric space $(A,d_A)$
64:alone, so that a set compact in one ambient space is compact in every other one
65:containing it isometrically.
66:
67:**The empty space is compact**, since the empty subfamily of any family covers
68:it; this is the reason the clause above is written with the two cases. The
69:one-point space is compact too, and so is every space listed as
70:$\{x_0, \dots, x_n\}$: given a cover, each $x_i$ lies in some member, and
71:finitely many members chosen in this way already cover.
72:
73:**The finiteness convention, and how it is used both ways.** "Finite" above is
74:the listing form, matching the finite lists of
75:[[def-finite-intersection-property]]. It agrees with the definition of
76:finiteness by equinumerosity with a natural number ([[def-countable]]), and
77:both directions of the agreement are available and are used below:
78:
79:- A nonempty finite set $F$ in the sense of [[def-countable]] satisfies
80:  $F \approx m$ for some $m \ge 1$, and a bijection $m \to F$ is exactly a
81:  listing $F = \{a_0, \dots, a_{m-1}\}$.
82:- Conversely a set listed as $A = \{a_0, \dots, a_n\}$, that is the image of a
83:  function $a$ with domain $\sigma(n)$, is finite in the sense of
84:  [[def-countable]]: the map sending $x \in A$ to the least $i \le n$ with
85:  $a_i = x$ is an injection of $A$ into $\sigma(n)$, so $A$ is equinumerous with
86:  a subset of $\mathbb{N}$ bounded above, and such a subset is finite
87:  ([[lem-subset-of-countable]]).
88:
89:Neither direction uses a choice principle: the second selects nothing, taking a
90:least index instead.
91:

FILE items/def-standard-topologies.md
43:## Definition
44:
45:Throughout, a topology is as in [[def-topological-space]], and *finite*,
46:*at most countable* and *uncountable* are as in [[def-countable]], so that
47:"countable" always means "at most countable" and every finite set is countable.
48:Let $X$ be a set. The six families below are topologies on $X$; that each really
49:satisfies (T1), (T2) and (T3) is discharged in full after the list.
50:
51:1. **Discrete topology.** $\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{disc}} := \mathcal{P}(X)$: every
52:   subset is open, hence every subset is closed, hence every subset is clopen.
53:2. **Indiscrete topology.** $\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{ind}} := \{\varnothing, X\}$.
54:   Its closed sets are again $\varnothing$ and $X$.
55:3. **Cofinite topology.**
56:   $\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{cof}} := \{\varnothing\} \cup \{\, U \subseteq X : X \setminus U \text{ is finite} \,\}$.
57:   Its closed sets are $X$ together with the finite subsets of $X$.
58:4. **Cocountable topology.**
59:   $\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{coc}} := \{\varnothing\} \cup \{\, U \subseteq X : X \setminus U \text{ is at most countable} \,\}$.
60:   Its closed sets are $X$ together with the at most countable subsets of $X$.
61:5. **Particular-point topology.** Fix $p \in X$ and put
62:   $\mathcal{T}_p := \{\varnothing\} \cup \{\, U \subseteq X : p \in U \,\}$: the
63:   open sets are $\varnothing$ and the sets containing $p$. Its closed sets are
64:   $X$ together with the sets **not** containing $p$.
65:6. **Sierpinski topology.** On a two-point set $S = \{a, b\}$ with $a \ne b$,
66:   $\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{Sier}} := \{\varnothing, \{b\}, S\}$. The pair
67:   $(S, \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{Sier}})$ is **Sierpinski space**; $b$ is its open
68:   point and $a$ its closed point. This is exactly the particular-point topology
69:   of item 5 on a two-point set with particular point $b$, listed separately
70:   because it is quoted so often.
71:
72:**Two elementary facts about finite sets are used below, and both are proved
73:here.**
74:
75:*(i) A subset of a finite set is finite.* Let $F \approx n$ with $n \in \mathbb{N}$
76:([[def-equinumerous]], [[def-natural-numbers]]), witnessed by a bijection
77:$\varphi : F \to n$, and let $B \subseteq F$. Then $\varphi$ restricts to a
78:bijection of $B$ onto $\varphi[B] \subseteq n$
79:([[def-injection-surjection-bijection]]). Every element of the von Neumann natural
80:$n$ is a natural number strictly smaller than $n$
81:([[lem-nat-order-is-membership]]), so $\varphi[B]$ is a subset of $\mathbb{N}$
82:bounded above by $n$, hence finite by the sharper form of
83:[[lem-subset-of-countable]] ("a subset $S \subseteq \mathbb{N}$ is finite if it is
84:bounded above"). Since $\approx$ is symmetric and transitive, $B$ is finite.
85:
86:*(ii) A union of two finite sets is finite.* First, if $H$ is finite and $g$ is
87:any object then $H \cup \{g\}$ is finite: if $g \in H$ there is nothing to prove,
88:and otherwise a bijection $u : H \to k$ extends to a bijection
89:$H \cup \{g\} \to k \cup \{k\} = \sigma(k)$ by setting $u(g) := k$, which is
90:injective because $k \notin k$ ([[lem-nat-transitive-irreflexive]]). Now fix a
91:finite set $F$ and argue by induction ([[thm-induction-principle]]) on
92:$m \in \mathbb{N}$ over the statement "for every $G$ with $G \approx m$, the union
93:$F \cup G$ is finite". At $m = 0$ we have $G = \varnothing$ and $F \cup G = F$. At
94:$m = \sigma(j)$, a bijection $\psi : G \to \sigma(j)$ gives $g := \psi^{-1}(j)$
95:and $G' := G \setminus \{g\} \approx j$ (restrict $\psi$), so
96:$F \cup G = (F \cup G') \cup \{g\}$ is finite by the induction hypothesis and the
97:previous sentence.
98:
99:**Discharge of the topology axioms.**
100:
101:*Discrete.* Every subset of $X$ lies in $\mathcal{P}(X)$, so (T1), (T2) and (T3)
102:hold with nothing to check.
103:
104:*Indiscrete.* (T1) is the definition. For (T2), a subfamily of
105:$\{\varnothing, X\}$ has union $\varnothing$ (if it is empty or $\{\varnothing\}$)
106:or $X$ (otherwise). For (T3), $\varnothing \cap A = \varnothing$ and
107:$X \cap X = X$.
108:
109:*Cofinite.* (T1): $\varnothing$ is listed, and $X \setminus X = \varnothing$ is
110:finite. (T2): let $\mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{cof}}$. If every
111:member is $\varnothing$ the union is $\varnothing$. Otherwise fix
112:$U_0 \in \mathcal{S}$ with $U_0 \ne \varnothing$; then
113:$X \setminus \bigcup \mathcal{S} \subseteq X \setminus U_0$, which is finite, so
114:the left side is finite by (i). (T3): for nonempty $U, V$ with finite
115:complements, $X \setminus (U \cap V) = (X \setminus U) \cup (X \setminus V)$ is
116:finite by (ii); and if either of $U, V$ is empty so is $U \cap V$. The closed sets
117:are the complements of the open ones, that is $X = X \setminus \varnothing$
118:together with the finite sets.
119:
120:*Cocountable.* Identical to the cofinite case with "at most countable" in place of
121:"finite": (i) is replaced by [[lem-subset-of-countable]] itself, and (ii) by the
122:statement that a union of two at most countable sets is at most countable, which
123:is the two-set instance of [[thm-countable-union-of-countable]] applied to the
124:family $A_0 := U, A_1 := V, A_k := \varnothing$ for $k \ge 2$.
125:
126:*Particular point.* (T1): $\varnothing$ is listed and $p \in X$. (T2): a
127:subfamily whose members are all $\varnothing$ has union $\varnothing$; otherwise
128:some member contains $p$, hence so does the union. (T3): if $U$ and $V$ both
129:contain $p$ then so does $U \cap V$; and if either is $\varnothing$ then so is the
130:intersection.
131:
132:*Sierpinski.* The special case $X = \{a,b\}$, $p = b$ of the previous paragraph:
133:the sets containing $b$ are $\{b\}$ and $S$, so
134:$\mathcal{T}_b = \{\varnothing, \{b\}, S\} = \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{Sier}}$.
135:

FILE items/thm-compactness-agrees-with-metric-compactness.md
41:## Statement
42:
43:Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space ([[def-metric-space]]) and let $\mathcal{T}_d$ be
44:its metric topology ([[def-metric-topology]]), so that $(X, \mathcal{T}_d)$ is a
45:topological space ([[def-topological-space]]) and is metrizable
46:([[def-metrizable-space]]). Then:
47:
48:1. $(X,d)$ is a compact metric space ([[def-metric-compactness]]) **if and only
49:   if** $(X, \mathcal{T}_d)$ is a compact topological space
50:   ([[def-compact-space]]).
51:2. For every $A \subseteq X$: $A$ is a compact subset of the metric space
52:   $(X,d)$ if and only if $A$ is a compact subset of the topological space
53:   $(X, \mathcal{T}_d)$, the two readings of "compact subset" being the metric
54:   subspace $(A, d_A)$ ([[def-isometry-and-metric-embedding]]) and the topological
55:   subspace $(A, (\mathcal{T}_d)_A)$ ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]).
56:
57:**Nothing here is a coincidence and nothing is transported.** The open-cover
58:condition of [[def-metric-compactness]] quantifies over families of subsets open
59:in $(X,d)$, and by [[def-metric-topology]] those are exactly the members of
60:$\mathcal{T}_d$; so the two conditions are not merely equivalent, they are the
61:same condition written twice. No choice principle is used.
62:

FILE items/def-metric-compactness-variants.md
34:## Definition
35:
36:Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space ([[def-metric-space]]), with open sets as in
37:[[def-metric-topology]] and open covers, subcovers, finiteness and compactness as
38:in [[def-metric-compactness]].
39:
40:- $(X,d)$ is **countably compact** when every open cover $\mathcal{U}$ of $X$
41:  that is at most countable ([[def-countable]]) has a finite subcover.
42:- $(X,d)$ is **sequentially compact** when every sequence $(x_k)$ in $X$, that is
43:  every function $x : \mathbb{N} \to X$ ([[def-sequence]]), has a subsequence
44:  $(x_{n_j})$ converging to a point of $X$ ([[def-metric-convergence]]), the
45:  index map $n : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$ being strictly increasing
46:  ([[lem-index-map-grows]]).
47:- $(X,d)$ is **limit point compact** when every infinite subset $A \subseteq X$
48:  has a limit point in $X$, that is a point $p \in X$ with
49:  $B(p,r) \cap (A \setminus \{p\}) \ne \emptyset$ for every real $r > 0$
50:  ([[def-metric-interior-closure-boundary]]). Here *infinite* means not finite in
51:  the sense of [[def-countable]], equivalently not listable as
52:  $\{a_0, \dots, a_n\}$ and not empty ([[def-metric-compactness]]).
53:
54:A subset $A \subseteq X$ is called countably compact, sequentially compact or
55:limit point compact when the metric subspace $(A, d_A)$ is
56:([[def-isometry-and-metric-embedding]]), exactly as for compactness.
57:
58:**The countable covers may be listed.** A nonempty at most countable family
59:$\mathcal{U}$ admits a surjection $\mathbb{N} \to \mathcal{U}$
60:([[lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n]]), so countable compactness says: for
61:every sequence $(U_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ of open sets with
62:$X = \bigcup_{n \in \mathbb{N}} U_n$ there are finitely many indices whose sets
63:already cover $X$. That surjection is produced from the countability assumption
64:alone and no choice principle is involved; the empty family covers only the empty
65:space, which is compact anyway.
66:
67:**Limit points are computed where the set lives.** For $S \subseteq A \subseteq X$
68:and $a \in A$, the identity $B_A(a,r) = B_X(a,r) \cap A$
69:([[def-isometry-and-metric-embedding]]) shows that $a$ is a limit point of $S$ in
70:the subspace $(A,d_A)$ exactly when $a$ is a limit point of $S$ in $X$ and lies in
71:$A$. So "$A$ is limit point compact" says that every infinite $S \subseteq A$ has
72:a limit point **belonging to $A$**; a limit point outside $A$ does not count, and
73:that is what distinguishes the property from a statement about $X$.
74:

FILE items/def-metrizable-space.md
48:## Definition
49:
50:A topological space $(X, \mathcal{T})$ ([[def-topological-space]]) is
51:**metrizable** if there is a metric $d$ on $X$ ([[def-metric-space]]) whose
52:metric topology is $\mathcal{T}$, that is $\mathcal{T} = \mathcal{T}_d$
53:([[def-metric-topology]]). Such a $d$ is said to **induce** or **metrise**
54:$\mathcal{T}$.
55:
56:**The definition presupposes that $\mathcal{T}_d$ is a topology in the sense of
57:[[def-topological-space]], and it is.** By [[def-metric-topology]] both
58:$\varnothing$ and $X$ are open in $(X,d)$, which is (T1), and by
59:[[thm-metric-open-set-algebra]] the family $\mathcal{T}_d$ is closed under
60:arbitrary unions, which is (T2), and under intersections of $n \ge 1$ members,
61:which contains (T3). So every metric space is a topological space, and the
62:metric-space development of this library is a special case of the present one.
63:
64:**The standard local notions in the two developments agree after translating
65:their neighbourhood conventions.** Let $d$ be a metric on $X$ and give $X$ the
66:topology $\mathcal{T}_d$.
67:
68:- *Neighbourhoods and balls.* [[def-metric-topology]] uses "neighbourhood" for
69:  an open set containing $x$, whereas [[def-neighbourhood-top]] also allows a
70:  non-open superset of such a set. Thus the two collections are not literally
71:  equal, but the open metric neighbourhoods are cofinal in the broader
72:  neighbourhood filter. The balls $B(x,r)$, $r > 0$ ([[def-metric-ball]]), are
73:  open ([[thm-metric-open-set-algebra]]) and form a neighbourhood base at $x$:
74:  any neighbourhood $N$ contains an open $U \ni x$, hence a ball around $x$ by
75:  [[def-metric-topology]]. The balls of radius $1/n$ alone already suffice
76:  ([[lem-metric-ball-neighbourhood-base]]).
77:- *Interior, closure, boundary.* [[def-metric-interior-closure-boundary]] defines
78:  them by the same conditions with balls in place of neighbourhoods, and the
79:  previous bullet makes the two conditions equivalent; the metric closure is the
80:  smallest closed superset ([[thm-metric-closure-characterisation]]), which is the
81:  definition used here ([[def-interior-closure-boundary-top]]). So the two
82:  closures, the two interiors and the two boundaries are the same three
83:  operations.
84:- *Convergence.* $x_k \to p$ in the sense of [[def-sequence-convergence-top]] is
85:  "eventually in every neighbourhood of $p$", and by the first bullet this is
86:  "eventually in every ball around $p$", which is [[def-metric-convergence]].
87:- *Continuity.* $\varepsilon$-$\delta$ continuity at $a$
88:  ([[def-metric-continuity]]) says that every ball around $f(a)$ has a ball
89:  around $a$ mapped into it, which by the first bullet is continuity at $a$ in
90:  the sense of [[def-continuous-map-top]].
91:
92:- *Subspaces.* For $A \subseteq X$ the subspace topology
93:  $\{\, U \cap A : U \in \mathcal{T}_d \,\}$ of [[def-subspace-topology-top]] is
94:  exactly the metric topology of the subspace metric $d_A$
95:  ([[def-isometry-and-metric-embedding]]), so the two uses of the phrase
96:  *subspace topology* in this library name one thing. Indeed
97:  $B_A(a,r) = B_X(a,r) \cap A$: a trace $U \cap A$ is $d_A$-open, since each of
98:  its points $a$ has $B_X(a,r) \subseteq U$ and hence $B_A(a,r) \subseteq U \cap A$;
99:  and a $d_A$-open $W$ is the trace of
100:  $U := \bigcup \{\, B_X(a,r) : a \in W,\ r > 0,\ B_X(a,r) \cap A \subseteq W \,\}$,
101:  which is $d_X$-open, satisfies $U \cap A = W$, and involves no choice principle,
102:  the union being taken over a set of pairs rather than over a selection.
103:
104:Consequently the metric-space notions of interior, closure, boundary, density,
105:convergence, continuity and subspace agree with the topological notions here,
106:and statements about them transfer once a metric is named. For neighbourhoods
107:the transfer uses the explicit convention change above: a metric-page
108:neighbourhood is an open topological neighbourhood, while every topological
109:neighbourhood contains one.
110:
111:**Metrizability is a topological property; the metric is not part of it.** If
112:$h : X \to Y$ is a homeomorphism ([[def-homeomorphism-and-open-maps]]) and $d$
113:metrises $X$, then $d'(y_1,y_2) := d(h^{-1}(y_1), h^{-1}(y_2))$ is a metric on
114:$Y$ — the three axioms transfer along the bijection $h^{-1}$ — and
115:$h[B_d(x,r)] = B_{d'}(h(x), r)$, so $h$ carries a basis of $\mathcal{T}_d$ to a
116:basis of $\mathcal{T}_{d'}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{d'} = h[\mathcal{T}_d] = \mathcal{T}_Y$.
117:Hence $Y$ is metrizable. The metric itself, however, is **not** determined by the
118:topology: two metrics on one set may induce the same topology without agreeing,
119:which is exactly topological equivalence ([[def-equivalent-metrics]]), and
120:properties of a metric that are not properties of its topology, boundedness among
121:them, are therefore not properties of a metrizable space.
122:
123:**Two things every metrizable space has.** It is **Hausdorff**: distinct points
124:have disjoint open neighbourhoods, by [[thm-metric-hausdorff-separation]] applied
125:to any metric inducing the topology. And it is **first countable**
126:([[def-first-countable-top]]), by [[lem-metric-ball-neighbourhood-base]]. Either
127:failure is therefore an obstruction to metrizability, and this page uses the
128:first of them to exhibit a topology induced by no metric.
129:
130:**Sequential limits in a metrizable space are unique, so the notation
131:$\lim_k x_k$ is available there.** In a metric space a sequence has at most one
132:limit ([[lem-metric-limits-unique]]), and by the agreement of convergence above
133:that uniqueness is a statement about the topology alone; so within a metrizable
134:space, and only there, this page writes $\lim_k x_k$ in the ordinary way. In a
135:general space the symbol is unavailable ([[def-sequence-convergence-top]]).
136:
137:**The usual topology of $\mathbb{R}$.** The absolute value makes $\mathbb{R}$ a
138:metric space under $d_{\mathbb{R}}(x,y) = |x-y|$, its open balls are the bounded
139:open intervals, and the resulting metric topology is what claim 3 of
140:[[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]] names the **usual topology** of $\mathbb{R}$.
141:That is the topology meant by the phrase throughout these two pages, and
142:$\mathbb{R}$ carrying it is metrizable by definition. Every statement about it on
143:these two pages is proved from the metric $d_{\mathbb{R}}$ and the bridge above,
144:and no example re-derives any of it.
145:

FILE items/def-metric-topology.md
37:## Definition
38:
39:Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space ([[def-metric-space]]).
40:
41:A subset $U \subseteq X$ is **open in $(X,d)$** if for every $x \in U$ there is a
42:real $r > 0$ with $B(x,r) \subseteq U$ ([[def-metric-ball]]). A subset
43:$F \subseteq X$ is **closed in $(X,d)$** if its complement $X \setminus F$ is
44:open.
45:
46:The collection
47:
48:$$\mathcal{T}_d := \{\, U \subseteq X : U \text{ is open in } (X,d) \,\}$$
49:
50:of all open subsets is the **metric topology** of $d$ on $X$. A subset of $X$
51:that is both open and closed is called **clopen**.
52:
53:**Two sets are open for trivial reasons.** $\emptyset$ is open, because the
54:defining condition quantifies over no points; and $X$ is open, because
55:$B(x,r) \subseteq X$ for every $x$ and every $r > 0$. Consequently $X$ and
56:$\emptyset$ are also closed, and both are clopen.
57:
58:**A neighbourhood of a point $x$** is any open set containing $x$. The condition
59:above therefore reads: $U$ is open exactly when every point of $U$ has a ball
60:around it inside $U$, and it is the balls alone that have to be tested.
61:
62:**The metric, not the set, determines $\mathcal{T}_d$.** Two metrics on the same
63:set may have different metric topologies, and two different metrics may have the
64:same one; the systematic comparison is [[def-equivalent-metrics]].
65:

FILE items/lem-compactness-is-intrinsic.md
35:## Statement
36:
37:Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space ([[def-metric-space]]), let $A \subseteq X$ and let
38:$(A,d_A)$ be the metric subspace ([[def-isometry-and-metric-embedding]]). Then:
39:
40:1. **Relative openness is a trace.** A set $V \subseteq A$ is open in $(A,d_A)$
41:   ([[def-metric-topology]]) if and only if $V = U \cap A$ for some $U$ open in
42:   $(X,d)$.
43:2. **Compactness read in the ambient space.** $A$ is a compact subset of $X$
44:   ([[def-metric-compactness]]), that is $(A,d_A)$ is a compact metric space, if
45:   and only if for every family $\mathcal{U}$ of open subsets of $X$ with
46:   $A \subseteq \bigcup \mathcal{U}$ there are $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and
47:   $U_0, \dots, U_n \in \mathcal{U}$ with $A \subseteq U_0 \cup \dots \cup U_n$,
48:   or else $A = \emptyset$.
49:3. **The same in indexed form.** $A$ is a compact subset of $X$ if and only if
50:   for every set $I$ and every family $(U_i)_{i \in I}$ of open subsets of $X$
51:   with $A \subseteq \bigcup_{i \in I} U_i$ there are $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and
52:   indices $i_0, \dots, i_n \in I$ with
53:   $A \subseteq U_{i_0} \cup \dots \cup U_{i_n}$, or else $A = \emptyset$.
54:
55:Claim 3 is the form used by almost every later proof on this page, because a
56:cover is usually produced by a rule that attaches an open set to each point or to
57:each index, and a set of open sets forgets that rule. No choice principle is used
58:anywhere below; the one place a selection is made is over a finite index set, and
59:[[lem-finite-choice]] is a theorem of ZF.
60:

