items/lem-dependent-choice-along-a-sequence-of-relations.md:21:    - title: "Axiom of dependent choice (Wikipedia)"
items/lem-dependent-choice-along-a-sequence-of-relations.md:22:      url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Axiom_of_dependent_choice"
items/lem-dependent-choice-along-a-sequence-of-relations.md:28:**Assume the Axiom of Dependent Choice** ([[def-dependent-choice]]).
items/lem-dependent-choice-along-a-sequence-of-relations.md:40:**Why this is not [[def-dependent-choice]] read off.** That axiom is stated for
items/lem-dependent-choice-along-a-sequence-of-relations.md:50:**Given:** A nonempty set $A$, a family $(R_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ of binary relations on $A$, a point $a_0 \in A$, and the Axiom of Dependent Choice.
items/lem-dependent-choice-along-a-sequence-of-relations.md:54:[L1] Dependent choice: for every nonempty set $X$, every relation $S$ on $X$ that is entire on $X$ — meaning every element of $X$ is related to some element of $X$ — and every $p \in X$, there is a function $x : \mathbb{N} \to X$ with $x_0 = p$ and $x_n \mathbin{S} x_{n+1}$ for every $n \in \mathbb{N}$ ([[def-dependent-choice]], [[def-sequence-convergence-top]]).
items/lem-dependent-choice-along-a-sequence-of-relations.md:76:**The device is the standard one and it is worth naming.** Carrying the stage as a first coordinate turns a family of relations into a single relation on a larger set, at the cost of having to check afterwards that the first coordinate really counts $0, 1, 2, \dots$; that check is step 4.1 and it is an ordinary induction on $\mathbb{N}$, not a second appeal to choice.
items/lem-dependent-choice-along-a-sequence-of-relations.md:78:**Nothing beyond dependent choice is spent.** The hypothesis [A1] is a pure existence statement, asserting for each stage and each element that *some* successor exists; it names none. All the selecting is done once, by [L1], and the lemma adds nothing to its cost.
