FILE items/thm-closed-subspace-of-a-compact-space-is-compact.md
39:## Statement
40:
41:Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]), with
42:subspaces as in [[def-subspace-topology-top]] and compactness as in
43:[[def-compact-space]]. Then:
44:
45:1. **Closed in compact is compact.** If $(X, \mathcal{T})$ is compact and
46:   $F \subseteq X$ is closed in $X$, then $F$ is a compact subset of $X$.
47:2. **Finite unions.** If $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and $K_0, \dots, K_n$ are compact
48:   subsets of $X$, then $K_0 \cup \dots \cup K_n$ is a compact subset of $X$. The
49:   union of the empty list is $\varnothing$, which is a compact subset of every
50:   space.
51:
52:Claim 1 needs $X$ to be compact and claim 2 does not; no hypothesis of any kind
53:is placed on $X$ in claim 2. **No choice principle is used**: claim 1 selects
54:nothing, taking a least index where a selection would be natural, and claim 2
55:makes finitely many selections through [[lem-finite-choice]], a theorem of ZF.
56:

FILE items/thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed.md
41:## Statement
42:
43:Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a Hausdorff topological space
44:([[def-hausdorff-space]], [[def-topological-space]]), with compact subsets as in
45:[[def-compact-space]]. Then:
46:
47:1. **A point and a disjoint compact set are separated.** If $K \subseteq X$ is
48:   compact and $x \in X \setminus K$, there are $U, V \in \mathcal{T}$ with
49:   $$x \in U, \qquad K \subseteq V, \qquad U \cap V = \varnothing .$$
50:2. **Two disjoint compact sets are separated.** If $K, L \subseteq X$ are compact
51:   and $K \cap L = \varnothing$, there are $U, V \in \mathcal{T}$ with
52:   $$L \subseteq U, \qquad K \subseteq V, \qquad U \cap V = \varnothing .$$
53:3. **Compact implies closed.** Every compact subset of $X$ is closed in $X$.
54:4. **In a compact Hausdorff space the two classes coincide.** If in addition
55:   $(X, \mathcal{T})$ is compact, then a subset of $X$ is compact if and only if
56:   it is closed.
57:
58:**The proof is written choice-free, and that is not a stylistic preference.** The
59:textbook argument says "for each $y \in K$ choose disjoint open $U_y, V_y$",
60:which is a selection over an arbitrary index set and therefore an appeal to the
61:full Axiom of Choice. What is done below instead is to take the family of **all**
62:open $V$ that admit some open $U \ni x$ disjoint from them — a family cut out by
63:a formula, with nothing selected — extract a finite subcover from it, and only
64:then make finitely many selections, which [[lem-finite-choice]] supplies as a
65:theorem of ZF.
66:

FILE items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md
41:## Statement
42:
43:Let $(X, \mathcal{T}_X)$ and $(Y, \mathcal{T}_Y)$ be topological spaces
44:([[def-topological-space]]), and let $\mathbb{R}$ carry its usual topology, the
45:metric topology of $d_{\mathbb{R}}(s,t) = |s-t|$
46:([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]], [[def-metric-topology]],
47:[[def-metrizable-space]]). Then:
48:
49:1. **Continuous images.** If $f : X \to Y$ is continuous
50:   ([[def-continuous-map-top]]) and $(X, \mathcal{T}_X)$ is compact
51:   ([[def-compact-space]]), then $f[X]$ is a compact subset of $Y$. More
52:   generally, if $K \subseteq X$ is a compact subset of $X$ then $f[K]$ is a
53:   compact subset of $Y$.
54:2. **Extreme values.** If $(X, \mathcal{T}_X)$ is compact and nonempty and
55:   $g : X \to \mathbb{R}$ is continuous, then $g[X]$ has a maximum and a minimum
56:   ([[def-max-min]]): there are $x_{\max}, x_{\min} \in X$ with
57:   $$g(x_{\min}) \;\le\; g(x) \;\le\; g(x_{\max}) \qquad \text{for every } x \in X .$$
58:3. **Compact to Hausdorff.** If $(X, \mathcal{T}_X)$ is compact, $(Y,
59:   \mathcal{T}_Y)$ is Hausdorff ([[def-hausdorff-space]]) and $f : X \to Y$ is a
60:   continuous bijection, then $f$ is a homeomorphism
61:   ([[def-homeomorphism-and-open-maps]]).
62:
63:Nonemptiness in claim 2 is a hypothesis and not an oversight: for
64:$X = \varnothing$ the image is empty and has neither a maximum nor a minimum. **No
65:choice principle is used**: the one selection made below is over a finite index
66:set, where [[lem-finite-choice]] is a theorem of ZF.
67:

FILE items/lem-tube-lemma-for-a-compact-factor.md
40:## Statement
41:
42:Let $(X, \mathcal{T}_X)$ and $(Z, \mathcal{T}_Z)$ be topological spaces
43:([[def-topological-space]]) and give $X \times Z$ the product topology
44:([[def-product-topology]]). Let $K \subseteq X$ be a compact subset
45:([[def-compact-space]]), let $z_0 \in Z$, and let $N \subseteq X \times Z$ be open
46:with
47:
48:$$K \times \{z_0\} \;\subseteq\; N .$$
49:
50:Then there is an open $W \subseteq Z$ with $z_0 \in W$ and
51:
52:$$K \times W \;\subseteq\; N .$$
53:
54:The set $K \times W$ is the **tube** of the name. The case $K = \varnothing$ is
55:included and is settled by $W = Z$. **No choice principle is used at all**: the
56:cover produced below is indexed by *pairs* of open sets, so the indexed form of
57:the ambient compactness criterion ([[lem-compactness-of-a-subspace-is-ambient]],
58:claim 2) returns the second entries together with the indices and nothing has to
59:be selected afterwards.
60:

FILE items/thm-finite-products-of-compact-spaces.md
42:## Statement
43:
44:For every $n \in \mathbb{N}$ ([[def-natural-numbers]]) and every family
45:$(X_k)_{k < n}$ of compact topological spaces ([[def-compact-space]],
46:[[def-topological-space]]), the product
47:
48:$$\prod_{k < n} X_k$$
49:
50:with the product topology ([[def-product-topology]]) is compact. In particular a
51:binary product $X \times Y$ of compact spaces is compact, and the empty product,
52:a one-point space, is compact.
53:
54:**No choice principle is used beyond [[lem-finite-choice]]**, which is a theorem
55:of ZF. That is what separates the finite case from the arbitrary one, where the
56:Axiom of Choice is genuinely spent.
57:

FILE items/cor-heine-borel-in-the-product-topology.md
38:## Statement
39:
40:Let $n \in \mathbb{N}$ with $n \ge 1$, let $\mathbb{R}^n$ be the set of functions
41:$n \to \mathbb{R}$ ([[lem-metrics-on-rn]]) carrying the product topology of $n$
42:copies of the usual topology of $\mathbb{R}$ ([[def-product-topology]]), and let
43:$d_2$ be the Euclidean metric. Then:
44:
45:1. The product topology on $\mathbb{R}^n$ **is** the metric topology of $d_2$
46:   ([[def-metric-topology]]), so $\mathbb{R}^n$ as a product and $\mathbb{R}^n$ as
47:   a metric space are one topological space, and it is metrizable
48:   ([[def-metrizable-space]]).
49:2. A subset $K \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$ is a compact subset for the product
50:   topology ([[def-compact-space]]) **if and only if** $K$ is closed in
51:   $\mathbb{R}^n$ and bounded ([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]).
52:
53:The hypothesis $n \ge 1$ is inherited from [[lem-metrics-on-rn]], which defines
54:$\mathbb{R}^n$ and its three metrics only there; for $n = 0$ the product is a
55:one-point space and is compact. **No choice principle is used**: the metric
56:statement it is read off from is proved by bisection
57:([[thm-heine-borel-rn]]).
58:

FILE items/thm-locally-compact-hausdorff-basics.md
42:## Statement
43:
44:Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]). Then:
45:
46:1. **A neighbourhood base of compact sets.** If $X$ is locally compact
47:   ([[def-locally-compact-space]]) and Hausdorff ([[def-hausdorff-space]]), then
48:   every neighbourhood $N$ of a point $x \in X$ ([[def-neighbourhood-top]])
49:   contains a compact neighbourhood of $x$; so the compact neighbourhoods of $x$
50:   form a neighbourhood base at $x$.
51:2. **Heredity along open and closed subspaces.** If $X$ is locally compact and
52:   Hausdorff and $S \subseteq X$ is open, then the subspace $S$ is locally
53:   compact ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]). If $X$ is locally compact and
54:   $F \subseteq X$ is closed, then the subspace $F$ is locally compact; no
55:   Hausdorff hypothesis is used for this half.
56:3. **Shrinking inside an open set.** If $X$ is locally compact and Hausdorff,
57:   $O \subseteq X$ is open and $x \in O$, there is an open $V$ with
58:   $$x \in V \subseteq \overline{V} \subseteq O$$
59:   and $\overline{V}$ a compact subset of $X$.
60:4. **Compact sets sit in open sets with compact closure.** If $X$ is locally
61:   compact and Hausdorff and $K \subseteq X$ is compact, there is an open
62:   $V \subseteq X$ with $K \subseteq V$ and $\overline{V}$ a compact subset of
63:   $X$ ([[def-interior-closure-boundary-top]]).
64:
65:**No choice principle is used**; every cover produced below is defined by a
66:formula and thinned by [[lem-compactness-of-a-subspace-is-ambient]], which returns
67:members rather than indices.
68:

FILE items/thm-one-point-compactification-properties.md
40:## Statement
41:
42:Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]) and let
43:$(X^{*}, \mathcal{T}^{*})$ be its one-point compactification, with added point
44:$\infty$ ([[def-one-point-compactification]]). Then:
45:
46:1. **$X^{*}$ is compact** ([[def-compact-space]]).
47:2. **$X$ is an open subspace of $X^{*}$**: $X \in \mathcal{T}^{*}$, and the
48:   subspace topology that $X$ inherits from $X^{*}$
49:   ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]) is $\mathcal{T}$ itself.
50:3. **$X$ is dense in $X^{*}$** ([[def-dense-top]]) **if and only if $X$ is not
51:   compact.**
52:4. **$X^{*}$ is Hausdorff** ([[def-hausdorff-space]]) **if and only if $X$ is
53:   locally compact** ([[def-locally-compact-space]]) **and Hausdorff.**
54:
55:In particular, a locally compact Hausdorff space is an open subspace of a compact
56:Hausdorff space, which is the reason the construction is made. **No choice
57:principle is used**: the only cover thinned below is thinned by the indexed form
58:of [[lem-compactness-of-a-subspace-is-ambient]], which returns its own indices.
59:

FILE items/lem-the-order-topology-on-an-ordinal.md
38:## Statement
39:
40:Let $\gamma$ be an ordinal ([[def-ordinal]]), regarded as the set of ordinals
41:below it, linearly ordered by membership ([[lem-ordinal-trichotomy]]), and give it
42:the order topology ([[def-order-topology-on-a-linearly-ordered-set]]). For
43:$\alpha, \beta \in \gamma$ write
44:
45:$$[0,\beta] := \{\, \xi \in \gamma : \xi \le \beta \,\}, \qquad (\alpha,\beta] := \{\, \xi \in \gamma : \alpha < \xi \le \beta \,\} .$$
46:
47:Then:
48:
49:1. Every set of either form is **clopen** ([[def-topological-space]]), and
50:   $$\mathcal{B}_\gamma \;:=\; \{\, [0,\beta] : \beta \in \gamma \,\} \;\cup\; \{\, (\alpha,\beta] : \alpha, \beta \in \gamma \,\}$$
51:   is a basis for the order topology of $\gamma$
52:   ([[def-topology-basis-subbasis]]).
53:2. The isolated points of $\gamma$ ([[def-interior-closure-boundary-top]]) are
54:   exactly the ordinals $\xi \in \gamma$ that are $0$ or a successor; a limit
55:   ordinal $\xi \in \gamma$ ([[def-limit-ordinal]]) is not isolated.
56:3. $\gamma$ with its order topology is Hausdorff ([[def-hausdorff-space]]).
57:
58:**Regularity is not claimed here**, and nothing below asserts any separation
59:property beyond claim 3; the finer separation axioms are not available at this
60:point in the reading order.
61:

FILE items/thm-quasicomponents-equal-components-in-a-compact-hausdorff-space.md
42:## Statement
43:
44:Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a compact Hausdorff space ([[def-compact-space]],
45:[[def-hausdorff-space]], [[def-topological-space]]) and let $x \in X$. Then the
46:quasicomponent $Q(x)$ is connected
47:([[def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent]], [[def-connected-space]]), and
48:consequently
49:
50:$$C(x) \;=\; Q(x) :$$
51:
52:the component of $x$ and the quasicomponent of $x$ are the same set, so the
53:components and the quasicomponents of $X$ are the same family of subsets.
54:
55:The inclusion $C(x) \subseteq Q(x)$ holds in every space
56:([[thm-quasicomponents-contain-components]], claim 1) and can be strict; what the
57:two hypotheses buy is the reverse inclusion. **No choice principle is used.**
58:

FILE items/thm-alexander-subbase-lemma.md
32:## Statement
33:
34:**Assume the Axiom of Choice** ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]), in the form of Zorn's
35:lemma ([[thm-zorn]]), the two being equivalent over ZF ([[cor-ac-iff-zorn]]).
36:
37:Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]) and let
38:$\mathcal{S}$ be a subbasis for $\mathcal{T}$
39:([[def-topology-basis-subbasis]]). Suppose that
40:
41:> every family $\mathcal{S}_0 \subseteq \mathcal{S}$ with
42:> $X = \bigcup \mathcal{S}_0$ has a finite subfamily whose union is $X$.
43:
44:Then $(X, \mathcal{T})$ is compact ([[def-compact-space]]).
45:
46:The converse is immediate and is not the content: a compact space has a finite
47:subcover for **every** open cover, subbasic or not. What the lemma says is that
48:the subbasic covers alone already decide compactness, and that is what makes it
49:usable — a product topology is presented by a subbasis, and the subbasic covers
50:of a product are far easier to handle than its arbitrary open covers.
51:

FILE items/thm-tychonoff.md
43:## Statement
44:
45:**Assume the Axiom of Choice** ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]).
46:
47:Let $I$ be a set and let $(X_i, \mathcal{T}_i)_{i \in I}$ be a family of compact
48:topological spaces ([[def-compact-space]], [[def-topological-space]]). Then the
49:product
50:
51:$$P \;:=\; \prod_{i \in I} X_i$$
52:
53:with the product topology ([[def-product-topology]]) is compact.
54:
55:**The Axiom of Choice is spent twice, and both uses are flagged below.** Once
56:inside [[thm-alexander-subbase-lemma]], through Zorn's lemma ([[thm-zorn]]), and
57:once directly at step 2.1, to produce a point of a product of nonempty sets.
58:

FILE items/fs-compact-implies-sequentially-compact.md
40:## Statement
41:
42:**False claim:** every compact topological space ([[def-compact-space]]) is
43:sequentially compact ([[def-compactness-variants]]).
44:
45:**Where the claim comes from, and what is actually true.** For a metric space the
46:two conditions are equivalent, and the claim above is that equivalence
47:transplanted to an arbitrary topological space. The refutation builds its own
48:witness out of Tychonoff's theorem ([[thm-tychonoff]]): the product
49:
50:$$Y \;:=\; \prod_{x \in D} \{0,1\}, \qquad D := \{0,1\}^{\mathbb{N}},$$
51:
52:of one copy of the two-point discrete space for every $0$-$1$ sequence, together
53:with the sequence $(F_n)$ in $Y$ whose $n$-th term reads off the $n$-th
54:coordinate, $F_n(x) := x_n$. The Axiom of Choice is assumed, since
55:[[thm-tychonoff]] carries it.
56:

FILE items/fs-sequentially-compact-implies-compact.md
40:## Statement
41:
42:**False claim:** every sequentially compact topological space
43:([[def-compactness-variants]]) is compact ([[def-compact-space]]).
44:
45:**Where the claim comes from, and what is actually true.** For a metric space the
46:two conditions are equivalent, and that equivalence is proved elsewhere in this
47:library at a stated choice cost; the claim above is that equivalence transplanted
48:to an arbitrary topological space, where it fails. What does hold in general is
49:only that sequential compactness implies countable compactness, and that at the
50:cost of countable choice.
51:
52:**The refutation assumes the Axiom of Countable Choice**
53:([[def-countable-choice]]), because that is what makes the witness sequentially
54:compact; without it the witness is not known to have the property the claim would
55:have to preserve. The witness is $\omega_1$ with its order topology
56:([[def-first-uncountable-ordinal]], [[lem-the-order-topology-on-an-ordinal]]).
57:

FILE items/fs-countably-compact-implies-compact.md
40:## Statement
41:
42:**False claim:** every countably compact topological space
43:([[def-compactness-variants]]) is compact ([[def-compact-space]]).
44:
45:**Where the claim comes from, and what is actually true.** Countable compactness
46:tests only the at most countable open covers, and the claim above asserts that
47:testing those is enough. It is enough when the space is also Lindel&ouml;f, and
48:the claim above drops that hypothesis.
49:
50:**The refutation assumes the Axiom of Countable Choice**
51:([[def-countable-choice]]), which is what makes each witness countably compact.
52:Two witnesses are available and both are refutations on their own: the first
53:uncountable ordinal $\omega_1$ with its order topology
54:([[def-first-uncountable-ordinal]], [[lem-the-order-topology-on-an-ordinal]]), and
55:the closed long ray ([[def-the-long-line]]).
56:

FILE items/thm-baire-category-locally-compact-hausdorff.md
33:## Statement
34:
35:**Assume the Axiom of Dependent Choice** ([[def-dependent-choice]]).
36:
37:Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a locally compact Hausdorff space
38:([[def-locally-compact-space]], [[def-hausdorff-space]],
39:[[def-topological-space]]). Then $X$ is a Baire space ([[def-baire-space]]): for
40:every sequence $(U_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ of dense open subsets of $X$
41:([[def-dense-top]], [[def-sequence-convergence-top]]), the intersection
42:$\bigcap_{n \in \mathbb{N}} U_n$ is dense in $X$.
43:
44:**Dependent choice is sufficient here and no claim of necessity is made.** The
45:several statements that go by the name "Baire category theorem" are inequivalent
46:over ZF, and the choice principles they correspond to differ; that account,
47:including the fact that the compact Hausdorff version is equivalent to a
48:principle strictly weaker than dependent choice, is
49:[[rem-baire-category-choice-strength]], which this library states and does not
50:prove. Nothing below asserts that dependent choice is needed for the statement
51:above.
52:

FILE items/lem-dependent-choice-along-a-sequence-of-relations.md
26:## Statement
27:
28:**Assume the Axiom of Dependent Choice** ([[def-dependent-choice]]).
29:
30:Let $A$ be a nonempty set and let $(R_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ be a family of binary
31:relations on $A$, indexed by $\mathbb{N}$ ([[def-natural-numbers]]), such that
32:
33:$$\text{for every } n \in \mathbb{N} \text{ and every } u \in A \text{ there is } v \in A \text{ with } u \mathbin{R_n} v .$$
34:
35:Then for every $a_0 \in A$ there is a function $a : \mathbb{N} \to A$
36:([[def-sequence-convergence-top]]) with
37:
38:$$a(0) = a_0 \qquad \text{and} \qquad a(n) \mathbin{R_n} a(n+1) \ \text{ for every } n \in \mathbb{N} .$$
39:
40:**Why this is not [[def-dependent-choice]] read off.** That axiom is stated for
41:**one** relation $R$, entire on one set, fixed before any step is taken. What is
42:needed here is a relation that changes with the stage: at step $n$ the admissible
43:successors are the $R_n$-successors, and $R_n$ is a different relation for each
44:$n$. A family of relations on $A$ is not a relation on $A$, so the axiom does not
45:apply to it directly, and applying it as if it did would be a genuine gap. The
46:proof below removes the gap by carrying the stage inside the set.
47:

FILE items/fs-infinite-has-countable-subset-in-zf.md
38:## Statement
39:
40:**FALSE.** The statement
41:
42:> every infinite set has a countably infinite subset
43:
44:is a theorem of ZF: it can be proved from the Zermelo-Fraenkel axioms without
45:any choice principle.
46:
47:Here "infinite" means "not finite" and "countably infinite" means "equinumerous
48:with $\mathbb{N}$" ([[def-countable]], [[def-equinumerous]]). The claim is
49:plausible because the proof everyone reaches for seems to need nothing at all:
50:$A$ is infinite, so it is nonempty, so pick $a_0 \in A$; then $A \setminus \{a_0\}$
51:is still infinite, so pick $a_1$; and so on, giving
52:$\{a_0, a_1, a_2, \dots\} \approx \mathbb{N}$. The "and so on" is the whole
53:difficulty. Each step depends on the previous choices and there are infinitely
54:many of them, so what the argument uses is dependent choice
55:([[def-countable-choice]] records where DC sits), not a construction. Nothing in
56:ZF turns "$A$ is not equinumerous with any natural number" into a rule for
57:naming elements of $A$.
58:

