    41	---
    42	
    43	The **ultrafilter lemma** (UL), that every filter on a set extends to an
    44	ultrafilter ([[thm-ultrafilter-lemma]]), is a genuine choice principle. It is
    45	neither free nor as expensive as the Axiom of Choice, and this remark records
    46	exactly where it sits, separating what this library proves from what it cites.
    47	
    48	**What is proved here.** The Axiom of Choice implies UL. That is
    49	[[thm-ultrafilter-lemma]], which uses Zorn's lemma, and Zorn's lemma is
    50	equivalent to the Axiom of Choice over ZF ([[cor-ac-iff-zorn]],
    51	[[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Nothing else about the strength of UL is derived in
    52	this library, and the three statements below are cited, not proved.
    53	
    54	**What is cited and not proved.**
    55	
    56	- **UL is not a theorem of ZF.** If ZF is consistent, ZF does not prove that a
    57	  free ultrafilter on $\mathbb{N}$ exists (Feferman 1965, by forcing:
    58	  [[rem-feferman-no-free-ultrafilter-in-zf]]), and UL produces one by extending
    59	  the filter of tails, so ZF does not prove UL. This is external to this library
    60	  exactly as the independence of the Axiom of Choice itself is
    61	  ([[fs-zorn-provable-in-zf]]).
    62	- **UL does not imply the Axiom of Choice.** If ZF is consistent, there is a
    63	  model of ZF in which UL holds and the Axiom of Choice fails (Halpern and Lévy
    64	  1971: [[rem-halpern-levy-bpi-not-ac]]). Together with the previous point this
    65	  places UL strictly between the two, again under the consistency of ZF: it is
    66	  not provable in ZF, and it is not strong enough to recover AC.
    67	- **UL is the Boolean prime ideal theorem.** Over ZF, UL is equivalent to the
    68	  statement that every nontrivial Boolean algebra has a prime ideal, equivalently
    69	  an ultrafilter in the lattice sense. The dictionary is the one visible in
    70	  [[lem-ultrafilter-prime]]: a maximal filter is a prime filter, and complements
    71	  turn filters into ideals. The Boolean form is the name under which the
    72	  principle is catalogued in the literature on weak choice principles.
    73	
    74	**Why this matters for the rest of the library.** A theorem proved from UL is not
    75	"a theorem of choice" in the same sense as one proved from the Axiom of Choice.
    76	Because the Axiom of Choice and Zorn's lemma are equivalent, a proof through Zorn
    77	shows the theorem costs **at most** the Axiom of Choice, and nothing more than
    78	that: it is an upper bound on the price, never a lower one ([[cor-ac-iff-zorn]]).
    79	A result provable from UL alone carries the strictly smaller upper bound UL, and a
    80	page that nonetheless routes it through Zorn is overpaying and should say so. UL
    81	itself is the standing example, proved here from Zorn and yet, on the cited
    82	results above and so under the consistency of ZF, strictly weaker than the Axiom
    83	of Choice. That is why
    84	[[thm-ultrafilter-lemma]] is kept as a named statement rather than being inlined
    85	into its applications: naming it is what makes the smaller bound visible
    86	downstream.
    87	
    88	**Two honest caveats.**
    89	
    90	- This library proves the implication AC $\Rightarrow$ UL and nothing about the
    91	  converse direction. Calling UL "strictly weaker" is a citation, and it depends
    92	  on the consistency of ZF, which is not provable inside ZF.
    93	- The implication proved here goes through Zorn's lemma, so the *proof* given in
    94	  [[thm-ultrafilter-lemma]] uses full choice even though the *statement* does not
    95	  require it. A proof of UL from a weaker principle would not change the theorem,
    96	  only its price. Nothing in this library currently avoids Zorn's lemma at that
    97	  step.
