    24	      url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baire_category_theorem"
    25	    - title: "C. E. Blair, The Baire category theorem implies the principle of dependent choices, Bull. Acad. Polon. Sci. 25 (1977), 933-934"
    26	      url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baire_category_theorem"
    27	    - title: "J. Fossy and M. Morillon, The Baire category property and some notions of compactness, J. London Math. Soc. 57 (1998), 1-19"
    28	      url: "https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-the-london-mathematical-society/article/abs/baire-category-property-and-some-notions-of-compactness/5C6265B552C8D43D21F16C60802D6109"
    29	    - title: "A. Blass, Injectivity, projectivity, and the axiom of choice, Trans. Amer. Math. Soc. 255 (1979), 31-59 (Section 7: DMC, 'every tree has a subtree whose levels are finite')"
    30	      url: "https://doi.org/10.1090/S0002-9947-1979-0542870-6"
    31	    - title: "E. Tachtsis, The Urysohn Lemma is independent of ZF + Countable Choice, Proc. Amer. Math. Soc. 147 (2019), 4029-4038 (introduction: DMC is strictly weaker than each of DC and MC)"
    32	      url: "https://doi.org/10.1090/proc/14590"
    33	pipeline_run: null
    34	---
    35	
    36	## Statement
    37	
    38	Over ZF, "the Baire category theorem" is not one statement. Naming the versions
    39	by the class of spaces they quantify over:
    40	
    41	**(BCT-metric)** In a complete metric space, a countable intersection of dense
    42	open sets is dense. **Over ZF this is equivalent to the axiom of dependent
    43	choice (DC).** The implication from DC is the usual proof, in which each nested
    44	ball is chosen in terms of the previous one; the converse is Blair (1977).
    45	
    46	**(BCT-separable)** The same statement restricted to **separable** complete
    47	metric spaces **is a theorem of ZF**, with no choice principle at all: a fixed
    48	countable dense set removes every choice from the construction.
    49	
    50	**(BCT-compact-Hausdorff)** Every compact Hausdorff space is a Baire space. Over
    51	ZF this is equivalent to the principle "every tree has a subtree all of whose
    52	levels are finite", introduced by Blass (1979) and known as **dependent multiple
    53	choice (DMC)**. The equivalence is Fossy and Morillon (1998).
    54	
    55	**Separating them.** DMC is strictly weaker than each of DC and the axiom of
    56	multiple choice, in ZF and in ZFA alike, so BCT-metric and BCT-compact-Hausdorff
    57	are genuinely different assumptions. The Boolean prime ideal theorem does not
    58	imply DMC either, and so does not give BCT-compact-Hausdorff. The ground for that
    59	last point is recorded elsewhere on this page: DMC implies Urysohn's lemma
    60	(Blass 1979) while BPI does not (Brunner 1983, in the Mostowski linearly ordered
    61	model). The same pair shows DMC is not itself a theorem of ZF, since Urysohn's
    62	lemma is not.
    63	
    64	**If ZF is consistent**, none of these principles is a theorem of ZF except
    65	BCT-separable, and the non-implications listed are witnessed by models.
    66	
    67	## Remarks
    68	
    69	- **Where the DMC separations are recorded.** The two facts about DMC used in
    70	  "Separating them" are the ones stated in
    71	  [[rem-urysohn-lemma-not-a-zf-theorem]]: Blass's implication from DMC to
    72	  Urysohn's lemma, and Brunner's model in which BPI holds and Urysohn's lemma
    73	  fails.
    74	
    75	- **Not proved in this library.** No direction of any of these equivalences is
    76	  proved here, and the models witnessing the non-implications are symmetric
    77	  extensions and permutation models that this library does not build.
    78	
    79	- **What would prove it.** For the equivalences: a careful ZF development of
    80	  metric and compact Hausdorff spaces together with the combinatorics of trees.
    81	  For the separations: permutation models with the Jech-Sochor or Pincus transfer
    82	  theorems, that is, the same track as [[rem-cohen-forcing-ac-independent]].
    83	
    84	- **Why it matters here.** Every use of the Baire category theorem in this
    85	  library must name its version and pay for it. It is easy to write "by Baire" and
    86	  quietly consume DC where the separable case would have been free, and easy to
    87	  claim the locally compact or compact Hausdorff case is choice-free when it is
    88	  not. The accounting pages [[rem-choice-ledger]] and [[rem-choice-strengths]]
    89	  record principles by name for exactly this reason, and
    90	  [[def-countable-choice]] is where the weakest of the relevant principles is
    91	  defined. What is **not** affected is availability: nothing about the Baire
    92	  category theorem needs measure theory or functional analysis, so the theorem is
    93	  in scope; only its cost is recorded here.
    94	
    95	- **A variant not asserted here.** The locally compact regular form of the
    96	  theorem is sometimes attributed to Bacsich (1972) with the same equivalence.
    97	  That attribution was not confirmed against a primary source, so this item does
    98	  not state it.
    99	
   100	- **Conditional discipline.** The unprovability and non-implication clauses are
   101	  relative to the consistency of ZF. The two equivalences are ordinary ZF
   102	  theorems and need no consistency hypothesis; they are on this page because
   103	  their proofs, not their status, are out of reach here.
