    33	
    34	## What this page spends, implication by implication
    35	
    36	[[thm-metric-compactness-equivalences]] states five conditions and asserts that
    37	they are equivalent, under two choice hypotheses. Stated that way the theorem
    38	overcharges almost every arrow it contains, so this remark records the arrows one
    39	at a time. Every entry is a statement about **the proof given in this library**,
    40	and about nothing else.
    41	
    42	**Theorems of ZF, using no choice principle at all.**
    43	
    44	- A compact subset of a metric space is closed and bounded
    45	  ([[thm-compact-subset-is-closed-and-bounded]]).
    46	- A compact metric space is complete and totally bounded
    47	  ([[thm-compact-implies-complete-and-totally-bounded]]). Completeness is
    48	  obtained there from the finite intersection characterisation applied to the
    49	  closures of the tails of a Cauchy sequence, precisely so that the argument does
    50	  not pass through the extraction of a subsequence.
    51	- Compactness implies countable compactness and limit point compactness, and each
    52	  of those implies sequential compactness
    53	  ([[thm-compact-implies-the-other-compactness-forms]]). The two arrows into
    54	  sequential compactness extract a subsequence by taking, at every stage, the
    55	  **least** admissible index.
    56	- A sequentially compact metric space is complete
    57	  ([[lem-sequentially-compact-implies-complete]]).
    58	- A closed subset of a compact metric space is compact, a continuous image of a
    59	  compact space is compact, the extreme value theorem, the Lebesgue number lemma,
    60	  Heine-Cantor, and the continuity of the inverse of a continuous bijection from
    61	  a compact space.
    62	- Heine-Borel in $\mathbb{R}^n$ ([[thm-heine-borel-rn]]), by a bisection in which
    63	  each step halves one coordinate and keeps the left half when the left half is
    64	  still not finitely covered.
    65	
    66	**Using the Axiom of Countable Choice** ([[def-countable-choice]]), spent once and
    67	named at the step that spends it.
    68	
    69	- A complete, totally bounded metric space is compact
    70	  ([[thm-complete-and-totally-bounded-implies-compact]]): one finite
    71	  $1/(n+1)$-net, together with a listing of it, is fixed for every $n$ at once.
    72	- A compact metric space has an at most countable dense subset
    73	  ([[lem-compact-metric-space-has-a-countable-dense-subset]]): the same
    74	  selection, and the countable union theorem it then invokes carries the same
    75	  hypothesis and no more.
    76	
    77	**Using the Axiom of Dependent Choice** ([[def-dependent-choice]]).
    78	
    79	- A sequentially compact metric space is totally bounded
    80	  ([[thm-sequentially-compact-implies-totally-bounded]]). This is the only
    81	  implication on the page with that cost. The construction adds one point at a
    82	  time, each at distance at least $\varepsilon$ from all the points already
    83	  produced, so the set the next point is drawn from is not known until the
    84	  earlier ones are fixed. Countable choice returns one $\varepsilon$-separated
    85	  tuple for each length with no coherence between them, and no diagonal argument
    86	  assembles those into a single separated sequence.
    87	
    88	## What is claimed and what is not
    89	
    90	**Claimed:** each proof in this library can be carried out in ZF together with
    91	the principle named above, and in no case is more used than is named.
    92	
    93	**Not claimed:** that any of these principles is *necessary*. Showing that an
    94	implication cannot be proved in ZF alone is an independence result, obtained by
    95	forcing or by permutation models, and this library contains neither and proves
    96	none. Every cost above is an upper bound. The systematic study of which forms of
    97	compactness need which fragment of choice is a subject in its own right, and
    98	Herrlich's *Axiom of Choice* is the standard reference; it is cited here as
    99	literature and is not used.
   100	
   101	**Not claimed either:** that a cost recorded for one proof is a cost of the
   102	statement. Two proofs of the same implication may spend differently, and the
   103	completeness half of
   104	[[thm-compact-implies-complete-and-totally-bounded]] is exactly a case where the
   105	textbook route and the route taken here differ in what they use.
   106	
   107	## How to read the equivalence theorem
   108	
   109	A cycle of implications transmits the weakest hypothesis around the whole cycle:
   110	once [[thm-metric-compactness-equivalences]] has closed its cycle, every one of
   111	its five conditions implies every other *under both hypotheses*. The individual
   112	arrows do not inherit that. A reader working in ZF alone still has, without any
   113	choice at all, that a compact metric space satisfies all four of the other
   114	conditions, and that a sequentially compact one is complete. What fails in ZF, as
   115	far as this library's proofs go, is the journey back from the weaker conditions to
   116	compactness.
   117	
   118	Where these principles sit relative to one another — that the Axiom of Choice
   119	implies dependent choice, which implies countable choice, and that the reverse
   120	implications are relative-consistency results quoted rather than proved — is
   121	recorded in [[def-dependent-choice]] and in the definitions it points to.

