    26	---
    27	
    28	**Objective.** Compactness is the hypothesis under which an infinite covering
    29	argument collapses to a finite one. This page defines it for an arbitrary
    30	topological space, proves what it gives — closedness in a Hausdorff space,
    31	separation of disjoint compact sets, attainment of extreme values, automatic
    32	continuity of an inverse — and settles how it behaves under the constructions of
    33	general topology: subspaces, products of any size, continuous images, and the
    34	adjunction of a single point at infinity.
    35	
    36	**Compactness of a subset is intrinsic.** [[def-compact-space]] defines a compact
    37	space by open covers and calls a subset compact when the subspace it carries is
    38	compact. That is a statement about the topological subspace, so it is invariant
    39	under homeomorphism. For the same underlying set in another ambient, the answer
    40	is guaranteed to agree when the induced topology agrees. The bridge to
    41	the working form is [[lem-compactness-of-a-subspace-is-ambient]]: a subset is
    42	compact exactly when every family of ambient open sets covering it has finitely
    43	many members that already cover it, in the indexed form as well as the unindexed
    44	one. Almost every proof below uses the indexed form, because a cover is normally
    45	produced by a rule attaching an open set to each point or to each index, and a
    46	bare set of open sets forgets the rule. The same reading was already fixed for
    47	metric spaces, and [[thm-compactness-agrees-with-metric-compactness]] shows that
    48	the two developments describe one notion, which is what makes the whole metric
    49	theory of compactness available here once a metric inducing the topology is named.
    50	
    51	**What compactness gives.** [[thm-compact-iff-fip]] converts the covering
    52	condition into a statement about closed sets with the finite intersection
    53	property, which is the form used whenever a common point has to be produced.
    54	[[thm-closed-subspace-of-a-compact-space-is-compact]] and
    55	[[thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed]] are the two halves of the
    56	relationship between compactness and closedness, and the second carries more than
    57	its name: in a Hausdorff space a point and a disjoint compact set, and two
    58	disjoint compact sets, lie in disjoint open sets. Those separation clauses are
    59	proved **choice-free**, by collecting the family of all open sets that work
    60	rather than choosing one for each point, and they are what later pages use to
    61	separate closed sets in a compact Hausdorff space.
    62	[[thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps]] collects the payoff: a continuous image
    63	of a compact space is compact, a continuous real-valued function on a nonempty
    64	compact space attains a maximum and a minimum, and a continuous bijection from a
    65	compact space to a Hausdorff space is a homeomorphism.
    66	
    67	**Products.** [[lem-tube-lemma-for-a-compact-factor]] is the step that makes a
    68	product of two compact spaces compact, and it is proved with no choice principle
    69	at all by indexing its cover with pairs of open sets;
    70	[[thm-finite-products-of-compact-spaces]] runs the induction. For an arbitrary
    71	index set the argument changes character: [[thm-alexander-subbase-lemma]] reduces
    72	compactness to covers by members of a fixed subbasis, using Zorn's lemma, and
    73	[[thm-tychonoff]] then follows because a subbasic cover of a product restricts
    74	one coordinate at a time. Tychonoff's theorem implies the Axiom of Choice, so no
    75	proof of it can be free.
    76	
    77	**Weaker cousins, and what each implication costs.**
    78	[[def-compactness-variants]] introduces countable compactness, the Lindelöf
    79	property, sequential compactness, limit point compactness, $\sigma$-compactness
    80	and relative compactness, and identifies those of them that the metric
    81	development had already defined. [[thm-compactness-variants-hierarchy]] proves the
    82	implications one at a time rather than as a single equivalence, because an
    83	equivalence proved round a cycle charges every arrow the maximum: compactness
    84	gives countable compactness, the Lindelöf property and limit point compactness in
    85	ZF; sequential compactness gives countable compactness at the cost of countable
    86	choice; countable compactness gives limit point compactness at the cost of
    87	dependent choice, and the converse holds when singletons are closed, again at
    88	the cost of countable choice.
    89	[[cor-heine-borel-in-the-product-topology]] records that on $\mathbb{R}^n$ the
    90	product topology is the Euclidean one, so Heine-Borel applies unchanged.
    91	
    92	**Local compactness and the Baire property.** [[def-locally-compact-space]] asks
    93	only that every point have a compact neighbourhood, and
    94	[[thm-locally-compact-hausdorff-basics]] shows what a Hausdorff hypothesis adds:
    95	a neighbourhood base of compact sets, the shrinking of any open set around a
    96	point to an open set with compact closure inside it, heredity along open and
    97	closed subspaces, and an open set with compact closure around any compact set.
    98	That shrinking clause is exactly what a nested construction needs, and
    99	[[thm-baire-category-locally-compact-hausdorff]] runs one: every locally compact
   100	Hausdorff space is a Baire space ([[def-baire-space]]), assuming dependent
   101	choice. The construction shrinks at stage $n$ into the $n$-th dense open set, so
   102	the relation governing it changes with the stage;
   103	[[lem-dependent-choice-along-a-sequence-of-relations]] is the bridge from the
   104	single-relation form of dependent choice to that situation, and it is needed
   105	because a family of relations is not a relation.
   106	
   107	**One point at infinity.** [[def-one-point-compactification]] adjoins a single
   108	point, constructed from the space rather than assumed to exist, and declares open
   109	the complements of the closed compact sets; the verification that this is a
   110	topology is carried out there.
   111	[[thm-one-point-compactification-properties]] proves that the result is compact,
   112	that the original space sits inside it as an open subspace with its own topology,
   113	that it is dense exactly when the original space is not compact, and that the
   114	result is Hausdorff exactly when the original space is locally compact and
   115	Hausdorff.
   116	
   117	**Ordinals and the long ray.** [[lem-the-order-topology-on-an-ordinal]] gives an
   118	ordinal space a basis of clopen sets and identifies its isolated points;
   119	[[thm-ordinal-spaces-and-compactness]] shows that a successor ordinal is compact
   120	and a limit ordinal is not, and that $\omega_1$ is sequentially compact and
   121	countably compact without being compact, assuming countable choice.
   122	[[thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact]] proves a Heine-Borel
   123	theorem for the closed initial segments of the long ray, using only order density
   124	and the least upper bound property, and deduces that the ray is not compact — a
   125	theorem of ZF — and, assuming countable choice, that it is countably compact and
   126	not Lindelöf. Both spaces are the standard witnesses for the
   127	false statements that close the page.
   128	[[thm-quasicomponents-equal-components-in-a-compact-hausdorff-space]] is the last
   129	theorem: in a compact Hausdorff space the quasicomponents are connected, so the
   130	two decompositions of a space into connected pieces agree.
   131	
   132	**The ledger.** [[rem-compactness-conventions-and-choice-ledger]] settles the
   133	conventions in force — that *compact* means the covering condition alone and
   134	never includes a Hausdorff hypothesis, that compactness of a subset is intrinsic,
   135	and that relative compactness is the one notion here that is not — and then
   136	records, result by result, which arguments are theorems of ZF and which spend the
   137	Axiom of Choice, countable choice or dependent choice. Every entry is a statement
   138	about the proof given here and never a claim that a choice principle is necessary.
   139	
   140	**False statements** close the page, each with a witness: that a compact
   141	subset is closed in every space, that compactness and sequential compactness
   142	imply each other, that countable compactness implies compactness, and that local
   143	compactness is hereditary. The worked witnesses, together with the compactness of
   144	the interval, the Cantor set and the Hilbert cube, are on the companion page.

