FILE items/def-locally-compact-space.md
41:## Definition
42:
43:A topological space $(X, \mathcal{T})$ ([[def-topological-space]]) is **locally
44:compact** when
45:
46:> every point of $X$ has a compact neighbourhood:
47:
48:that is, for every $x \in X$ there is a neighbourhood $N$ of $x$
49:([[def-neighbourhood-top]]) that is a compact subset of $X$
50:([[def-compact-space]], [[def-subspace-topology-top]]).
51:
52:**A neighbourhood need not be open here**, and that is what makes the condition
53:the weak one it is meant to be: $N$ is required only to contain some open set
54:containing $x$. Writing "compact **open** neighbourhood" instead would define a
55:strictly stronger property, satisfied by no space in which a point has no compact
56:open neighbourhood, $\mathbb{R}$ among them; and requiring the compact set merely
57:to *contain* $x$ would define a property so weak that every space with a
58:singleton has it, singletons being compact.
59:
60:**Every compact space is locally compact**, since $X$ itself is a neighbourhood
61:of each of its points and is a compact subset of itself. The converse fails, and
62:$\mathbb{R}^n$ is the standard witness.
63:
64:**What the condition says in a metric space.** Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space
65:carrying its metric topology ([[def-metric-topology]], [[def-metrizable-space]]),
66:with balls as in [[def-metric-ball]], and let $x \in X$. Then
67:
68:> $x$ has a compact neighbourhood **if and only if** there are a real $r > 0$ and
69:> a compact $K \subseteq X$ with $B(x,r) \subseteq K$.
70:
71:*Both directions are immediate and are discharged here.* If $N$ is a compact
72:neighbourhood of $x$, fix an open $U$ with $x \in U \subseteq N$; by
73:[[def-metric-topology]] there is $r > 0$ with $B(x,r) \subseteq U \subseteq N$, so
74:$K := N$ serves. Conversely, if $B(x,r) \subseteq K$ with $K$ compact, then $K$
75:contains the open set $B(x,r)$, which contains $x$, so $K$ is a neighbourhood of
76:$x$ and is compact. Compactness of a subset of $(X,d)$ means the same thing read
77:metrically and read topologically
78:([[thm-compactness-agrees-with-metric-compactness]]), so the criterion may be
79:applied with either development's theorems.
80:
81:**$\mathbb{R}^n$ is locally compact for every $n \ge 1$.** Give $\mathbb{R}^n$
82:the product topology, which is the metric topology of $d_\infty$
83:([[lem-metrics-on-rn]], [[cor-heine-borel-in-the-product-topology]]). For
84:$p \in \mathbb{R}^n$ the set
85:
86:$$Q_p \;:=\; \{\, x \in \mathbb{R}^n : d_\infty(x,p) \le 1 \,\}$$
87:
88:is closed, being the complement of the union of the open balls $B(y, d_\infty(y,p) - 1)$
89:over the points $y$ with $d_\infty(y,p) > 1$, and it is bounded
90:([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]), lying inside $B(p, 2)$; so $Q_p$ is compact by
91:[[cor-heine-borel-in-the-product-topology]]. It contains the open ball $B(p,1)$,
92:which contains $p$, so it is a compact neighbourhood of $p$. The space
93:$\mathbb{R}^n$ is not compact, so local compactness is strictly weaker than
94:compactness.
95:

FILE items/def-one-point-compactification.md
39:## Definition
40:
41:Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]).
42:
43:**A point outside $X$, named rather than assumed.** Put
44:
45:$$\infty \;:=\; \{\, y \in X : y \notin y \,\},$$
46:
47:a set by Separation. Then $\infty \notin X$: were $\infty \in X$, the defining
48:condition applied to $\infty$ itself would give
49:$\infty \in \infty \iff \infty \notin \infty$. So no hypothesis about $X$ is
50:needed to obtain a point outside it, and the construction below is available for
51:every space.
52:
53:**The space.** Put $X^{*} := X \cup \{\infty\}$ and
54:
55:$$\mathcal{T}^{*} \;:=\; \mathcal{T} \;\cup\; \{\, X^{*} \setminus C \;:\; C \subseteq X,\ C \text{ closed in } X \text{ and a compact subset of } X \,\} .$$
56:
57:The pair $(X^{*}, \mathcal{T}^{*})$ is the **one-point compactification**, or
58:**Alexandroff compactification**, of $X$. Members of $\mathcal{T}$ are said to be
59:of the **first kind** and the sets $X^{*} \setminus C$ of the **second kind**; a
60:set of the second kind is exactly an open set of $\mathcal{T}^{*}$ containing
61:$\infty$, since a member of $\mathcal{T}$ is a subset of $X$, and the set $C$ is
62:recovered from it as $C = X^{*} \setminus (X^{*} \setminus C)$.
63:
64:**$\mathcal{T}^{*}$ is a topology on $X^{*}$, and this is discharged here.**
65:Throughout, "closed" and "compact" without qualification mean closed in $X$ and a
66:compact subset of $X$ ([[def-compact-space]]); two facts about such sets are used
67:and both are [[thm-closed-subspace-of-a-compact-space-is-compact]]: a subset of a
68:compact $C$ that is closed in $X$ is closed in the subspace $C$
69:([[def-subspace-topology-top]]) and hence compact, and a union of two compact
70:subsets is compact.
71:
72:*(T1).* $\varnothing \in \mathcal{T}$, and $X^{*} = X^{*} \setminus \varnothing$
73:is of the second kind, $\varnothing$ being closed in $X$ and compact.
74:
75:*(T2).* Let $\mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{T}^{*}$, let $\mathcal{S}_1$ be the
76:members of $\mathcal{S}$ lying in $\mathcal{T}$ and $\mathcal{S}_2$ the rest, so
77:that every member of $\mathcal{S}_2$ is of the second kind. If
78:$\mathcal{S}_2 = \varnothing$ then $\bigcup \mathcal{S} = \bigcup \mathcal{S}_1$
79:lies in $\mathcal{T}$ by (T2) in $X$. Otherwise put
80:$U := \bigcup \mathcal{S}_1 \in \mathcal{T}$ and
81:$\mathcal{D} := \{\, X^{*} \setminus O : O \in \mathcal{S}_2 \,\}$, a nonempty
82:family of closed compact subsets of $X$, and $C_0 := \bigcap \mathcal{D}$. Then
83:$C_0$ is closed by (C2) of [[def-topological-space]], and it is a closed subset of
84:any one member of $\mathcal{D}$, hence compact. Now
85:
86:$$\bigcup \mathcal{S} \;=\; U \cup (X^{*} \setminus C_0) \;=\; X^{*} \setminus (C_0 \setminus U),$$
87:
88:and $C_0 \setminus U = C_0 \cap (X \setminus U)$ is closed in $X$ and a subset of
89:the compact $C_0$, hence compact; so $\bigcup \mathcal{S}$ is of the second kind.
90:
91:*(T3).* For $U, V \in \mathcal{T}$ the intersection lies in $\mathcal{T}$ by (T3)
92:in $X$. For two sets of the second kind,
93:$(X^{*} \setminus C) \cap (X^{*} \setminus D) = X^{*} \setminus (C \cup D)$, and
94:$C \cup D$ is closed in $X$ and compact as a union of two compact subsets. For one
95:of each, $\infty \notin U$ gives
96:$U \cap (X^{*} \setminus C) = U \cap (X \setminus C)$, an intersection of two
97:members of $\mathcal{T}$.
98:
99:**Why the compact sets are also required to be closed.** The complement of a
100:compact set that is not closed in $X$ would not make $\infty$'s neighbourhoods
101:behave: the union computation in (T2) uses that an intersection of the discarded
102:sets is again closed, and the intersection of arbitrary compact subsets of a
103:non-Hausdorff space need not be compact. When $X$ is Hausdorff every compact
104:subset is closed ([[thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed]]) and the
105:two descriptions agree, which is why many texts state the definition without the
106:word "closed" and silently assume the Hausdorff case.
107:

FILE items/def-hausdorff-space.md
37:## Definition
38:
39:A topological space $(X, \mathcal{T})$ ([[def-topological-space]]) is
40:**Hausdorff** when any two distinct points are separated by disjoint open sets:
41:for all $x, y \in X$ with $x \ne y$ there are $U, V \in \mathcal{T}$ with
42:
43:$$x \in U, \qquad y \in V, \qquad U \cap V = \varnothing .$$
44:
45:Since an open set containing a point is an open neighbourhood of it
46:([[def-neighbourhood-top]]), the condition reads: distinct points have disjoint
47:open neighbourhoods. Nothing is asserted about points that are equal, and the
48:condition is vacuous for a space with at most one point, so every such space is
49:Hausdorff.
50:
51:**Every metrizable space is Hausdorff.** This is not proved here, because it is
52:already discharged: [[def-metrizable-space]] records it among the two things
53:every metrizable space has, deriving it from
54:[[thm-metric-hausdorff-separation]], which separates $p \ne q$ in a metric space
55:by the disjoint open balls $B(p,r)$ and $B(q,r)$ with $r = d(p,q)/2 > 0$. In
56:particular $\mathbb{R}$ with its usual topology, every $\mathbb{R}^n$, and every
57:subspace of a metrizable space are Hausdorff.
58:
59:**Not every space is Hausdorff.** The indiscrete topology
60:$\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{ind}} = \{\varnothing, X\}$ on a set $X = \{a,b\}$ with
61:$a \ne b$ ([[def-standard-topologies]]) is not: the only open set containing $a$
62:is $X$, the only one containing $b$ is $X$, and $X \cap X = X \ne \varnothing$.
63:This is the same two-point space that [[def-metrizable-space]] uses to exhibit a
64:topology induced by no metric, and the reason is the same one: failure of the
65:Hausdorff condition is an obstruction to metrizability.
66:
67:**Being Hausdorff is a topological property** ([[def-homeomorphism-and-open-maps]]).
68:If $h : X \to Z$ is a homeomorphism and $X$ is Hausdorff, then for $z \ne z'$ in
69:$Z$ the points $h^{-1}(z)$ and $h^{-1}(z')$ are distinct, so they have disjoint
70:open $U, V$; the images $h[U]$ and $h[V]$ are open, disjoint, and contain $z$ and
71:$z'$ respectively, a homeomorphism carrying the open sets of one space
72:bijectively onto those of the other. So no space homeomorphic to a Hausdorff
73:space fails the condition.
74:
75:**Scope of this item.** Only the definition, the metrizable case and the
76:two-point failure are recorded here, because that is all this page uses. The
77:Hausdorff condition is one of a graded family of separation axioms; that family,
78:its ordering, and the questions of which of its members are hereditary or
79:preserved by products, are not available at this point in the reading order and
80:nothing here anticipates them. What this page does use is a single negative
81:result: a quotient of a Hausdorff space need not be Hausdorff, which is recorded
82:below as a false statement and witnessed on the companion page.
83:

FILE items/thm-zorn.md
43:## Statement
44:
45:Assume the Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Let $(P, \le)$ be a
46:nonempty poset in which **every chain has an upper bound**. Then $P$ has a
47:maximal element ([[def-maximal-element]]).
48:
49:Note the hypothesis asks only for an upper bound, not a least upper bound, and
50:the conclusion asserts only that a maximal element exists, never that a greatest
51:one does.
52:

FILE items/def-axiom-of-choice.md
41:## Definition
42:
43:The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.
44:
45:> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function
46:> ([[def-choice-function]]).
47:
48:Written out: for every set $\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,
49:there exists a function $g$ with domain $\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \in S$ for
50:all $S \in \mathcal{F}$.
51:
52:An equivalent formulation, immediate from the definition of the Cartesian
53:product, is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if $X_i \ne \emptyset$
54:for every $i \in I$, then $\prod_{i \in I} X_i \ne \emptyset$.
55:

