35:## Statement
36:
37:**Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]) and the Axiom
38:of Dependent Choice ([[def-dependent-choice]]).** Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space
39:([[def-metric-space]]). Then the following five conditions are equivalent.
40:
41:- **(a)** $(X,d)$ is compact ([[def-metric-compactness]]).
42:- **(b)** $(X,d)$ is countably compact ([[def-metric-compactness-variants]]).
43:- **(c)** $(X,d)$ is limit point compact.
44:- **(d)** $(X,d)$ is sequentially compact.
45:- **(e)** $(X,d)$ is complete ([[def-complete-metric-space]]) and totally bounded
46:  ([[def-totally-bounded]]).
47:
48:**The two hypotheses are not needed everywhere, and the statement should not be
49:read as if they were.** Of the implications assembled below, all but two are
50:theorems of ZF. Dependent choice is used only for "sequentially compact implies
51:totally bounded" ([[thm-sequentially-compact-implies-totally-bounded]]), and
52:countable choice only for "complete and totally bounded implies compact"
53:([[thm-complete-and-totally-bounded-implies-compact]]). Each is an upper bound on
54:the cost of the proof given in this library and not a claim of necessity; the
55:implication-by-implication account is
56:[[rem-compactness-choice-ledger-metric]].
57:

FILE items/thm-compact-implies-the-other-compactness-forms.md
36:## Statement
37:
38:Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space ([[def-metric-space]]), with compactness as in
39:[[def-metric-compactness]] and the three variants as in
40:[[def-metric-compactness-variants]]. Then:
41:
42:1. If $(X,d)$ is compact, it is countably compact.
43:2. If $(X,d)$ is compact, it is limit point compact.
44:3. If $(X,d)$ is countably compact, it is sequentially compact.
45:4. If $(X,d)$ is limit point compact, it is sequentially compact.
46:
47:**Every one of the four is a theorem of ZF.** Where a subsequence is extracted,
48:the index at each stage is the **least** admissible one, which
49:[[thm-well-ordering-principle]] makes canonical and [[thm-recursion]] then
50:assembles into a function; where finitely many indices have to be recovered from
51:finitely many sets, [[lem-finite-choice]] supplies them and is itself a theorem
52:of ZF. Nothing below appeals to countable or to dependent choice.
53:

