items/thm-compactness-variants-hierarchy.md:78:[L1] $X$ is compact when every open cover has a finite subcover; countably compact when every at most countable open cover has a finite subcover; Lindel&ouml;f when every open cover has an at most countable subcover; sequentially compact when every sequence has a convergent subsequence; limit point compact when every infinite subset has a limit point in $X$ ([[def-compact-space]], [[def-compactness-variants]]).
items/thm-compactness-variants-hierarchy.md:80:[L2] A finite family is at most countable, and *infinite* means not finite ([[def-countable]]).
items/thm-compactness-variants-hierarchy.md:82:[L3] $\overline{A} = A \cup A'$, where $A'$ is the set of limit points of $A$, and $A$ is closed exactly when $A = \overline{A}$; a limit point of a subset of $B$ is a limit point of $B$, since a neighbourhood meeting the smaller set meets the larger ([[thm-closure-characterisation-top]], claim 3; [[def-interior-closure-boundary-top]]).
items/thm-compactness-variants-hierarchy.md:84:[L4] $\varnothing$ and $X$ are open, unions of open sets are open, a union of finitely many closed sets is closed, and a set is closed exactly when its complement is open; a neighbourhood of a point contains an open set containing that point, and an open set containing a point is a neighbourhood of it ([[def-topological-space]], [[def-neighbourhood-top]]).
items/thm-compactness-variants-hierarchy.md:86:[L5] Countable choice: for every family $(Y_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets there is a function $f$ on $\mathbb{N}$ with $f(n) \in Y_n$ for every $n$ ([[def-countable-choice]]).
items/thm-compactness-variants-hierarchy.md:88:[L6] Dependent choice: for every nonempty set $S$, every relation $R$ entire on $S$ and every $a \in S$ there is a sequence $(s_k)$ in $S$ with $s_0 = a$ and $s_k \mathbin{R} s_{k+1}$ for every $k$ ([[def-dependent-choice]]).
items/thm-compactness-variants-hierarchy.md:90:[L7] A nonempty at most countable family admits a surjection from $\mathbb{N}$, so it may be listed as $(U_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ with repetitions allowed, and no choice principle is involved ([[lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n]], [[def-countable]]).
items/thm-compactness-variants-hierarchy.md:92:[L8] A sequence is a function on $\mathbb{N}$ and $\mathbb{N}$ contains $0$; $x_k \to p$ means $x_k$ lies in each neighbourhood of $p$ from some index on; and a strictly increasing index map satisfies $n_j \ge j$ ([[def-sequence]], [[def-sequence-convergence-top]], [[lem-index-map-grows]], [[def-natural-numbers]]).
items/thm-compactness-variants-hierarchy.md:94:[L9] A set is countably infinite exactly when it is equinumerous with $\mathbb{N}$, and the range of an injection $\mathbb{N} \to A$ is a countably infinite subset of $A$ ([[def-countable]], [[def-injection-surjection-bijection]]).
items/lem-compactness-of-a-subspace-is-ambient.md:59:[L1] A subset of $A$ is open in $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ exactly when it is the trace $U \cap A$ of a set $U$ open in $X$, this being the definition of the subspace topology ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]).
items/lem-compactness-of-a-subspace-is-ambient.md:61:[L2] $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ is compact exactly when every family of sets open in $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ whose union is $A$ has a finite subfamily whose union is $A$; a family is finite when it is empty or listable as $\{V_0, \dots, V_n\}$ ([[def-compact-space]]).
items/lem-compactness-of-a-subspace-is-ambient.md:63:[L3] A function with domain a natural number all of whose values are nonempty sets has a choice function, and this is a theorem of ZF ([[lem-finite-choice]]).

